---
/**
 * "Which platform are you backing up?" — the first block of the welcome page.
 *
 * THE LANDING IS WHERE A VISITOR DECLARES WHO THEY ARE, and it is the only screen where asking is
 * free. Everywhere else the platform filter lives quietly in the sidebar and starts with everything
 * on, because a reader mid-task should never be interrogated. Here nobody is mid-task yet, so three
 * cards are cheaper than making them find a chip later (Oleh, 2026-08-20).
 *
 * IT IS AN OFFER, NOT A GATE. Nothing below it is hidden until a card is pressed, and the page is
 * complete for someone who ignores it entirely. A landing that demands a choice before it will show
 * you anything is a landing that loses the person who does not know the answer yet.
 *
 * THE MARK CARRIES THE RECOGNITION and the vocabulary line carries the proof: a reader who works in
 * Bases and Tables knows instantly which card is theirs, before reading a word of the name.
 *
 * ── A CARD IS A DOOR, AND THAT IS NOW THE ONLY THING IT IS (Oleh, 2026-08-21) ────────────────
 *
 * It is a real `<a href>` to that platform's connecting page; the click handler only writes the
 * preference on the way out. So it works with the script blocked, opens in a new tab on a middle
 * click, and shows its target in the status bar.
 *
 * THE SECOND TREATMENT IS DELETED. Until today a `filter` variant drew the same three cards as
 * `<button>` selectors that narrowed in place, behind the switch at the foot of `LandingBody`. Dan
 * called it on the live portal — it will not work at three-plus platforms — and the measured reason
 * is stated once, in `landing/LandingBody.astro`: it was a SINGLE-SELECT control writing a
 * MULTI-SELECT preference, so at three-plus platforms the front page could say less than the
 * sidebar, the search modal, the chat and the roadmap, all of which are checkbox lists.
 *
 * WHAT THAT VARIANT WAS FOR — scoping without leaving the page — is now the shared `PlatformPicker`
 * in the hero above, which is that same multi-select. The two controls are DIFFERENT ACTS and both
 * are wanted: the picker answers "which platforms do I care about" and scopes; a card answers "take
 * me there now" and narrows only as the side effect of walking through it. What went with the
 * variant, deliberately: the `Show all three` widening control (the picker's own `None`/`All` is
 * that control now, and it is reachable from every page rather than only from this one) and the
 * `What we back up in X` foot link (step 1 of the path below already re-points itself at that
 * platform, and its other pages arrive in the documentation directory further down).
 */
import { FILTER_KEY } from '../lib/platforms';
import PlatformMark from './PlatformMark.astro';
/* Visitor-clickable surfaces use the DOCUMENTED subset, never the identity catalogue — an identity
   without a `connecting` page produces a card that 404s. See `lib/documented-platforms.ts`. */
import { DOCUMENTED_PLATFORMS, DOCUMENTED_PLATFORM_IDS } from '../lib/documented-platforms';
/* THE IDENTITY CATALOGUE IS READ HERE FOR ONE REASON AND IT IS NOT A VISITOR-FACING ONE: the two
   platforms that have no documentation tree are what the `?cards=` review parameter pads the strip
   with, so the layout above three can be looked at without writing documentation for a platform
   nobody has built. They render INERT and `hidden`, and nothing but that parameter reveals them. */
import { PLATFORMS } from '../lib/platforms';
import { CARDS_MAX, stacks } from '../lib/card-count';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';

/* NO AVAILABILITY ON THESE CARDS, and the flags that used to be here are gone (Oleh, 2026-08-20).
   For a fortnight each card read its own roadmap row and stamped ClickUp "In progress · Q1 2027"
   and Notion "Planned · Q3 2027", with a call to action of "Read the plan" instead of "Start
   here". The reasoning was sound and the premise was not: this portal is a DEMONSTRATION of what
   the finished product's documentation looks like, not a status board for the product. The same
   ruling took the provisional banners off the documentation pages, and these three chips were the
   last surface on the portal still contradicting it.
   What that costs, said plainly so nobody has to rediscover it: a visitor can now pick ClickUp and
   read a complete set of ClickUp pages for a thing they cannot buy yet. That is the demo, and the
   roadmap is still one click away from the header on every page. */

/* ── THE COUNT THE STRIP IS DRAWN AT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * `N` is the truth: one card per platform that has a `connecting` page, three today, and no URL
 * moves it. It is stamped on the section as `--ps-n` and as `data-ps-layout`, so the layout rule is
 * a function of the count rather than of the number three appearing in a stylesheet — which is the
 * bug this whole change exists to prevent, and the one the `Show all three` string already hit.
 *
 * `PAD` and `SLOTS` are the review padding, drawn `hidden` and never reachable by a visitor. PAD is
 * the real identities with no pages (Smartsheet, monday.com — real marks, real vocabularies, real
 * names). SLOTS are what is left to reach eight, and they are deliberately anonymous: inventing a
 * sixth brand to fill a row would put a product that does not exist on the front page of the
 * portal, which is the one thing no layout study is worth. They are numbered by POSITION.
 */
const N = DOCUMENTED_PLATFORMS.length;
const PAD = PLATFORMS.filter((p) => !DOCUMENTED_PLATFORM_IDS.includes(p.id));
const ORDINALS = ['one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five', 'six', 'seven', 'eight'];
const SLOTS = Array.from({ length: Math.max(0, CARDS_MAX - N - PAD.length) }, (_, i) => N + PAD.length + i + 1);

/* ── THE BLOCK FOLLOWS THE READER'S CHOICE, AND IT DOES IT IN CSS ────────────────────────────────
 * Oleh, 2026-08-21: once somebody has chosen their platforms in the hero picker, a card for a
 * platform they excluded has nothing to offer them. Nothing chosen draws every card (the newcomer's
 * first action, and the block carries the page there); one chosen draws that one; several draw
 * those. The block stopped being a QUESTION when the picker took that job — it is the way IN to the
 * platforms that are already yours, which is Dan's "quick access to filtered documentation for a
 * specific platform" exactly.
 *
 * IN CSS, KEYED TO ONE ROOT ATTRIBUTE, for the same reason everything else on this page is: the
 * pre-paint script in `LandingBody.astro` stamps `data-bo-platforms` BEFORE this markup is parsed,
 * so a returning reader never sees three cards collapse to one. A script that hid `<li>`s after
 * hydration would do exactly that, on this page's most important fold.
 *
 * THE RULES ARE GENERATED FROM `DOCUMENTED_PLATFORM_IDS`, not typed out. A hand-written triple is
 * how a fourth platform gets a card in the DOM that no rule can ever reveal — the defect this file
 * already carries a repaired instance of (E266). `is:global` because a scoped style block cannot
 * be built from a string, and the selectors below name only classes this file owns.
 *
 * `data-ps-plat` IS ON THE THREE REAL CARDS ONLY. The `?cards=` padding cards carry no such
 * attribute, so neither rule below can see them and the review parameter's `[hidden]` is never
 * fought over. Which is the tie-break, stated once: THE REVIEW PARAMETER WINS. `?cards=` exists to
 * compare LAYOUTS at N cards, so while it is in the address the narrowing is suspended entirely —
 * `LandingBody.astro`'s pre-paint script and `lib/landing-strip.ts` both bail on the PRESENCE of the
 * parameter, whatever its value, so the two readers cannot disagree about what suspends what.
 */
const NARROW_CSS = [
  ':root[data-bo-platforms] .ps-grid > li[data-ps-plat]{display:none}',
  ...DOCUMENTED_PLATFORM_IDS.map(
    (id) =>
      `:root[data-bo-platforms~="${id}"] .ps-grid > li[data-ps-plat="${id}"]{display:flex}`,
  ),
].join('\n');
---

<style set:html={NARROW_CSS}></style>

<section
  class="ps not-content"
  aria-labelledby="ps-h"
  data-platform-start
  data-storage-key={FILTER_KEY}
  data-ps-layout={stacks(N) ? 'stack' : 'row'}
  style={`--ps-n: ${N};`}
>
  {/* NOT THE SAME QUESTION AS THE HERO'S, and the wording has to earn that. The picker above asks
      which platforms you work in and scopes the portal to them; this asks where you want to GO
      next, and every card is a link that goes there. Two controls phrased as one question would
      read as the page asking twice and forgetting the answer.

      TWO PARAGRAPHS, ONE OF WHICH IS TRUE AT A TIME, and the second exists because the first became
      a lie the moment this block started following the choice: "nothing below is hidden until you
      do" describes a state a reader with a platform picked has already left — the filter is why
      they are looking at one card. Server-rendered and selected by the same root attribute as
      everything else here, so the correct sentence is the one that paints. */}
  <div class="ps-head">
    <h2 class="ps-h" id="ps-h">Start with your platform</h2>
    <p class="ps-sub ps-sub-open">
      Each card opens that platform's connecting guide and narrows the documentation to it. You can
      change it any time, and nothing below is hidden until you do.
    </p>
    <p class="ps-sub ps-sub-narrowed">
      Showing the platforms you picked. The documentation below follows the same choice — widen it
      any time with the picker above.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ul class="ps-grid">
    {
      DOCUMENTED_PLATFORMS.map((p) => (
        <li data-ps-plat={p.id}>
          <a
            class="ps-card"
            href={`/platforms/${p.id}/connecting/`}
            data-platform-start-pick={p.id}
          >
            <span class="ps-mark">
              <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={28} />
            </span>
            <span class="ps-text">
              <span class="ps-name">{p.name}</span>
              <span class="ps-vocab">
                {p.vocab.top}s, {p.vocab.mid}s, {p.vocab.row}s
              </span>
            </span>
            <span class="ps-go">
              Start here
              <Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} />
            </span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
    {
      PAD.map((p) => (
        <li hidden data-ps-pad>
          <span class="ps-card ps-inert">
            <span class="ps-mark">
              <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={28} />
            </span>
            <span class="ps-text">
              <span class="ps-name">{p.name}</span>
              <span class="ps-vocab">
                {p.vocab.top}s, {p.vocab.mid}s, {p.vocab.row}s
              </span>
            </span>
            <span class="ps-note">
              <Icon name="circle-dashed" size={14} />
              No pages yet
            </span>
          </span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
    {
      SLOTS.map((n) => (
        <li hidden data-ps-pad>
          <span class="ps-card ps-inert">
            <span class="ps-mark ps-mark-blank">
              <Icon name="circle-dashed" size={28} />
            </span>
            <span class="ps-text">
              <span class="ps-name">Platform {ORDINALS[n - 1]}</span>
              <span class="ps-vocab">Its own nouns, not yet known</span>
            </span>
            <span class="ps-note">
              <Icon name="circle-dashed" size={14} />
              Not a real product
            </span>
          </span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</section>

<script>
  import { mountPlatformStart } from '../lib/platform-start';
  import { mountCardCount } from '../lib/card-count';
  mountPlatformStart();
  /* REVIEW ONLY. It returns immediately unless `?cards=` is in the address, writes no preference
     and touches nothing outside this section. See the header of `lib/card-count.ts`. */
  mountCardCount();
</script>

<style>
  .ps {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
  }

  .ps-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .ps-h {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-20);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .ps-sub {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 38rem;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* ── THE ROW, SIZED BY A FLOOR RATHER THAN BY A NUMBER OF PLATFORMS ─────────────────────────
   * `repeat(3, …)` was here until 2026-08-21 and it was a fact about today typed into a stylesheet.
   * There are three documented platforms; there will be one before there are five, and five before
   * there are eight. The rule below is written in terms of the only two numbers that do not change:
   * the measure this block is laid out in, and the narrowest a card may be.
   *
   *   the measure   `LandingBody.astro` `.vb { max-width: 72rem }`  = 1152px
   *   the gap                                                       =   12px
   *   `--ps-card-min`, the floor                                    =  220px
   *
   * `auto-fit` makes as many tracks as fit at that floor, collapses the ones no card lands in, and
   * `1fr` then spreads the cards across the full measure — so N cards give N equal columns for every
   * N the row can hold, with no per-count rule and no breakpoint written down. At the measure that
   * is floor((1152 + 12) / (220 + 12)) = 5 columns, which is the arithmetic behind `ROW_MAX` in
   * `lib/card-count.ts` and the reason six cards STACK instead (see `[data-ps-layout='stack']`).
   *
   * THE `max-width` IS WHAT STOPS ONE CARD BECOMING A BANNER. Without it a single documented
   * platform would draw one 1152px-wide card with a 28px mark in the corner of it. The cap holds a
   * card at 376px — exactly the width it has at three, which is the width this block shipped with —
   * so one, two and three platforms all draw the SAME card and only the row gets longer. From four
   * the cap is bigger than the measure and the measure wins, so the cards divide it: 279px at four,
   * 220px at five.
   *
   * IT ALSO REPLACES THE 60rem BREAKPOINT that used to drop this grid to one column. The floor does
   * that job at any count and at any width — three cards need 3 x 220 + 24 = 684px of measure, so
   * they collapse to two columns and then to one on the way down to 390 without anything here
   * naming a device. NOTE, because it is a real change to a shipped surface: between roughly 700px
   * and 960px of measure the three cards now stay in a row where they used to be stacked.
   */
  .ps {
    --ps-card-min: 220px;
    --ps-card-max: 376px;
    --ps-gap: 12px;
  }

  .ps-grid {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(var(--ps-card-min), 100%), 1fr));
    gap: var(--ps-gap);
    /* `--ps-shown` IS THE NARROWED COUNT, and the cap has to follow it or the whole point of the
       cap is lost: `auto-fit` collapses the tracks the hidden cards left behind, so one visible
       card inside a three-card cap would stretch to the full 1152px measure and become the banner
       the `max-width` was written to prevent. `--ps-shown` is set on `<html>` by the pre-paint
       script and maintained by `lib/landing-strip.ts`; it is absent whenever the block is drawing
       everything, and `var(--ps-n)` — the true count, or the reviewed one `?cards=` wrote — is the
       fallback for that case. */
    max-width: calc(
      var(--ps-shown, var(--ps-n)) * var(--ps-card-max) +
        (var(--ps-shown, var(--ps-n)) - 1) * var(--ps-gap)
    );
  }

  .ps-grid > li {
    display: flex;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* AN AUTHOR `display` BEATS THE `hidden` ATTRIBUTE, and this app has already shipped one variant
     that stayed on screen because of exactly that. The rule above sets `display: flex` on every
     `li`, so the padding cards would be visible to every visitor without this line. */
  .ps-grid > li[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* A card rather than a chip, because this is the one place the choice is the subject of the
     screen rather than a control beside something else. Flat, hairline, one surface step: the same
     card the rest of this page uses, so it does not read as a promotion.

     ONE RULE SET FOR BOTH VARIANTS. The doors are anchors and the selectors are buttons, so the
     resets below (`font`, `text-align`, `appearance`) exist for the second and are inert for the
     first. Two rule sets would be two paints for one card, and they would drift. */
  .ps-card {
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 4px;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 20px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-12);
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    color: inherit;
    font: inherit;
    text-align: left;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition:
      border-color 150ms ease-out,
      background-color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .ps-card:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
  }

  .ps-card:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  .ps-mark {
    display: flex;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  /* The stack form needs the mark beside the words rather than above them, which means the words
     have to be one object. In the row form this column reproduces the spacing it replaces exactly:
     the card's own 4px gap used to separate the name from the nouns, and now this does. */
  .ps-text {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* A plain block since the availability chip beside it was removed; there is nothing left to lay
     out in a row. */
  .ps-name {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  /* The proof. Someone who works in Bases and Tables recognises their card here even if the mark
     and the name both failed them. */
  .ps-vocab {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .ps-go {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  /* Starlight's prose rhythm is a general-sibling rule and it reaches inside any nested flex or
     grid box: the first child keeps `margin-top: 0` and every sibling after it takes 16px. That is
     what put a row of buttons 16px below each other inside their own row once already.
     `not-content` on the section exempts the subtree; these are the belt to that pair of braces. */
  .ps > *,
  .ps-head > *,
  .ps-grid > li > *,
  .ps-card > *,
  .ps-text > *,
  .ps-note > * {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* ── ABOVE FIVE, THE ROW BECOMES A LIST ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * WHY NOT A HORIZONTAL SCROLLER, which was the other candidate. This is the one control on the
   * portal whose entire job is to show every platform that exists; a scroller answers "which
   * platforms are there?" with "some of them, swipe", and it does it at the exact moment a reader
   * whose platform is NOT on the list needs to be able to conclude that. It also puts the answer
   * behind a gesture on the page's most important fold.
   *
   * WHAT STACKING COSTS, and it is real: height, on that same fold. The compact row form is the
   * answer — mark, name and nouns on one line at ~48px instead of a ~150px card — so eight rows are
   * about 8 x 48 + 7 x 12 = 468px against 312px for two rows of eight cards. 156px, in exchange for
   * never hiding an option. That is the trade, stated so it can be overruled with a number.
   *
   * SIX IS THE THRESHOLD, and it is `ROW_MAX + 1` from `lib/card-count.ts` rather than a number
   * chosen here: at six the measure gives (1152 - 60) / 6 = 182px cards, 142px inside the padding,
   * where the longest vocabulary line breaks to three lines and the card stops reading as one
   * object. The switch is stamped as `data-ps-layout` by the server for the true count and by
   * `card-count.ts` for a reviewed one, so the stylesheet never counts anything itself.
   */
  /* ── AND AT EXACTLY ONE PLATFORM, THE SAME COMPACT ROW ───────────────────────────────────────
   * Oleh, 2026-08-21, looking at the one-card state: at one platform the block stops being a block
   * and becomes a single row — mark, name, nouns, `Start here →` — with no heading and no
   * paragraph above it. The heading answered itself ("Start with your platform", directly under a
   * hero that has just been told Airtable) and the paragraph described a filter the reader had
   * already applied. Three layers of packaging around one destination.
   *
   * IT IS LITERALLY THE STACK FORM, not a second visual language: the same four declarations,
   * reached by a second selector rather than copied. Eight platforms and one platform want the same
   * object for opposite reasons — eight because a card each would not fit, one because a card alone
   * is a poster — and the day that shape changes it must change for both.
   *
   * THE TRIGGER IS BOTH ATTRIBUTES TOGETHER. `data-bo-platform` alone means the reader named
   * exactly one platform, which is also true while `?cards=` is padding the strip for review;
   * `data-bo-platforms` is set only when the strip is actually narrowed. Together they mean "one
   * card is being drawn, and it is being drawn because of a choice". Both are stamped pre-paint by
   * `LandingBody.astro`, so the row is the first thing painted rather than a card that collapses.
   */
  .ps[data-ps-layout='stack'] .ps-grid,
  :root[data-bo-platform][data-bo-platforms] .ps-grid {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    max-width: none;
  }

  .ps[data-ps-layout='stack'] .ps-card,
  :root[data-bo-platform][data-bo-platforms] .ps-card {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 12px 16px;
  }

  .ps[data-ps-layout='stack'] .ps-mark,
  :root[data-bo-platform][data-bo-platforms] .ps-mark {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .ps[data-ps-layout='stack'] .ps-go,
  .ps[data-ps-layout='stack'] .ps-note,
  :root[data-bo-platform][data-bo-platforms] .ps-go {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-left: auto;
    flex: none;
  }

  /* THE HEAD GOES WITH IT. `display: none` and not a collapse: `.ps` is a 20px-gap flex column and
     a hidden child creates no gap, so the row sits where the heading was rather than 20px below a
     hole. The `aria-labelledby` on the section still names the heading, which is allowed to be
     hidden — a name is read from the referenced element's text, and losing it would leave a bare
     link in the document outline with nothing saying what it starts. */
  :root[data-bo-platform][data-bo-platforms] .ps-head {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── WHICH SENTENCE IS TRUE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .ps-sub-narrowed {
    display: none;
  }

  :root[data-bo-platforms] .ps-sub-open {
    display: none;
  }

  :root[data-bo-platforms] .ps-sub-narrowed {
    display: block;
  }

  /* ── REVIEW-ONLY PADDING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * Everything below paints the cards that only `?cards=` can reveal, and it goes out with
   * `lib/card-count.ts`. They are `<span>`s, not links or buttons, because neither has anywhere
   * honest to go: Smartsheet and monday.com have no documentation tree, so a door would 404 and a
   * selector would write a preference the sidebar renders no row to switch back off.
   */
  .ps-inert {
    cursor: default;
  }

  .ps-inert:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-base-300);
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
  }

  .ps-mark-blank {
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .ps-note {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* ── A CARD'S INNER PADDING AT NARROW IS 16 (2026-08-25) ──────────────────────────────────────
     `specs/16-responsive.md` §3, stated for the product and applied here: below the fold the card
     families converge on 16px inline and block. Measured at 390 before this rule, on the landing
     alone: `.ps-card` 20px put its label's left edge at 37 (12 gutter + 4 page pad + 1 border + 20)
     while `.ja-card`, `.vb-way` and the request rows right under it put theirs at 33. Two left edges
     four pixels apart on one scroll read as a mistake rather than as a step — which is the same
     sentence §3 uses, because it is the same defect.

     20 is right at the three-across reading, where the card is a poster. It is not right stacked.

     AT THE FOOT, after every base and stacked declaration of `.ps-card` — §10.1. The stacked layout
     already says `padding: 12px 16px`, which is a ROW rather than a card and is left alone. */
  @media (max-width: 50rem) {
    .ps-card { padding: 16px; }
  }

</style>
